problems.statement:
Recall that $\alpha_p : \Delta^p \to \Delta^n$ is the affine map sending the vertex $i$ to the vertex $i$ (for $0 \leq i \leq p$), and $\omega_q : \Delta^q \to \Delta^n$ is the affine map sending the vertex $j$ to the vertex $p + j$ (for $0 \leq j \leq q$). The _Alexander-Whitney map_

$$
\alpha_{X,Y} : S_*(X \times Y) \to S_*(X) \otimes S_*(Y)
$$

sends a simplex $\sigma : \Delta^n \to X \times Y$, with components $\sigma_1 : \Delta^n \to X$ and $\sigma_2 : \Delta^n \to Y$, to the sum

$$
\alpha(\sigma) = \sum_{p+q=n} (\sigma_1 \circ \alpha_p) \otimes (\sigma_2 \circ \omega_q) \,.
$$

(a) Check that this is a chain map.

(b) Check that it is "associative" and "unital":

```tikz
\usepackage{tikz-cd}
\begin{document}
\begin{tikzcd}
S_*(X \times Y \times Z) \arrow[r, "\alpha_{X \times Y, Z}"] \arrow[d, "\alpha_{X, Y \times Z}"'] & S_*(X \times Y) \otimes S_*(Z) \arrow[d, "\alpha_{X,Y} \otimes 1"] \\
S_*(X) \otimes S_*(Y \times Z) \arrow[r, "1 \otimes \alpha_{Y,Z}"'] & S_*(X) \otimes S_*(Y) \otimes S_*(Z)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{document}
```

and

```tikz
\usepackage{tikz-cd}
\begin{document}
\begin{tikzcd}
 & S_*(* \times X) \arrow[dl, "c"'] \arrow[d, "\alpha_{*,X}"] & & S_*(X \times *) \arrow[d, "\alpha_{X,*}"'] \arrow[dr, "c"] & \\
S_*(X) & S_*(*) \otimes S_*(X) \arrow[l, "\epsilon \cdot 1"] & & S_*(X) \otimes S_*(*) \arrow[r, "1 \cdot \epsilon"'] & S_*(X)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{document}
```

Here the map $c$ is induced from one of the projection isomorphisms $* \times X \to X$ or $X \times * \to X$,

$$
\epsilon \cdot 1 : S_*(*) \otimes S_*(X) \xrightarrow{\;\epsilon \otimes 1\;} \mathbb{Z} \otimes S_*(X) \xrightarrow{\;\cong\;} S_*(X)
$$

and $1 \cdot \epsilon$ is similar.

(c) Now observe that $\alpha$ is _not_ "commutative": give an example to show that

```tikz
\usepackage{tikz-cd}
\begin{document}
\begin{tikzcd}
S_*(X \times Y) \arrow[r, "S_*(T)"] \arrow[d, "\alpha_{X,Y}"'] & S_*(Y \times X) \arrow[d, "\alpha_{Y,X}"] \\
S_*(X) \otimes S_*(Y) \arrow[r, "\tau"'] & S_*(Y) \otimes S_*(X)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{document}
```

cannot commute for either sign in $\tau(x \otimes y) = \pm y \otimes x$.

(d) But show that this diagram _is_ naturally homotopy commutative, with

$$
\tau(x \otimes y) = (-1)^{pq} y \otimes x \,, \quad x \in S_p(X) \,, \quad y \in S_q(Y) \,.
$$

Construct a "semi-relative cross product," natural in $X$ and the pair $(Y, B)$:

$$
\times : H^p(X; R) \otimes_R H^q(Y, B; R) \to H^{p+q}(X \times Y, X \times B; R)
$$

that agrees with the cross product we constructed in class if $B = \varnothing$ and that makes ($R$ coefficients understood)

```tikz
\usepackage{tikz-cd}
\begin{document}
\begin{tikzcd}
H^p(X) \otimes H^q(B) \arrow[r, "\times"] \arrow[d, "1 \otimes \partial"'] & H^{p+q}(X \times B) \arrow[d, "\partial"] \\
H^p(X) \otimes H^{q+1}(Y, B) \arrow[r, "\times"'] & H^{p+q+1}(X \times Y, X \times B)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{document}
```

commute, at least up to sign. Give conditions under which it is an isomorphism.
